-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bounds for the number of sunny lines\n\nA sunny line cannot be parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.\nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most the points of the form\n\\((a,a)\\) that belong to \\(T_n\\); there are  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nsuch points, and the line \\(y=x\\) shows that this bound is sharp.\nHence a sunny line contains at most \\(m\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) such a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points\n(e.g. the line \\(x=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\)).\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) required lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\nThe total number of points that can be covered is therefore at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+k\\,m .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince all points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, (2) must be at least \\(|T_n|\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+k\\,m\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(m=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and solve (3) for \\(k\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) is even*, then \\(m=t\\) and (3) becomes  \n\n\\[\nn^2-kn+kt\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*, then \\(m=t+1\\) and (3) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Constructions attaining the admissible values\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), configurations that realise each value of \\(k\\) allowed by (6).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x+y=i+1\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny, they are distinct, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies\n\\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence it lies on exactly one of the \\(L_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nConsider the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of them, except the point\n\\((2,2)\\) (which is present because \\(n\\ge3\\)).\nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_4:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nNow all points are covered, the four lines are distinct, and exactly one of them\n(\\(L_4\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The case \\(k=n\\) ( \\(n\\) odd)\n\nWrite \\(n=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge1\\)).\nThe line \\(y=x\\) (sunny) contains the \\(t+1\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(t+1,t+1).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,t\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=-\\frac{i}{\\,t+1-i\\,}\\,x+(i+1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i+1,\\,t+1),\\qquad Q_i=(t+1,\\,i+1),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(T_n\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{i}{t+1-i}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\nThe \\(t\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\) together with the line \\(y=x\\) give \\(t+1=n\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are those with second coordinate equal to \\(t+2\\):\n\\[\n(1,t+2),(2,t+2),\\dots ,(t,t+2).\n\\]\nThese \\(t\\) points lie on the \\(t\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines\n\\(x+y=t+3,\\dots ,x+y=2t+2\\).\nThus we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, all of them sunny, covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  The case \\(k=n-1\\) ( \\(n\\) even)\n\nWrite \\(n=2t\\) (\\(t\\ge2\\)).\nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x+y=n+1\\quad\\text{(the diagonal }x+y=2t+1),\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_1\\) contains the \\(t\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,2t),\\;(2,2t-1),\\dots ,(t,t+1),\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nand \\(L_2\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are covered except the \\(t-1\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\dots ,(1,t-1)\n\\]\n\nand the \\(t-1\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(2,2),\\;(3,3),\\dots ,(t,t).\n\\]\n\nPair them as  \n\n\\[\n(1,i)\\;\\text{with}\\;(i+1,i+1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,t-1).\n\\]\n\nEach pair lies on the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{i+2}:\\;y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently all\n\\(t-1\\) lines \\(L_{3},\\dots ,L_{t+1}\\) are sunny.\nTogether with the two non‑sunny lines we have \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Non‑existence of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nInequality (6) shows that for even \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-1\\) and for odd\n\\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThe constructions of Sections 2.1–2.4 demonstrate that each integer\n\\(k\\) listed in the statement is indeed attainable.\nTherefore no other integer \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,n\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
