dd \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  \n   Define the following \\(k\\) sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{i}&:\\;y = x - i \\qquad &&(i=0,1,\\dots ,m),\\\\[2pt]\n   M_{j}&:\\;y = x + j \\qquad &&(j=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in (2) have slope \\(1\\); slope \\(1\\) is allowed, so every line in (2)\n   is sunny.  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) put \\(c=a-b\\).  \n   Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1=2m+2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   -(m)\\le c\\le m .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover,\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   c\\ge 0 &\\Longrightarrow\\ (a,b)\\text{ lies on }L_{c},\\\\[2pt]\n   c<0  &\\Longrightarrow\\ (a,b)\\text{ lies on }M_{-c}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines listed in\n   (2).  The lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n   Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines (2) cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n5. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n   * For any \\(n\\ge3\\) we may take \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-k\\) (all\n     non\u2011sunny) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines given by the construction above\n     (after translating the coordinates by the map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a+n-k,b)\\)).\n   * When \\(k=0\\) we simply use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n\\); all\n     are non\u2011sunny.\n   * When \\(k\\) is even and positive, the counting argument of step\u202f3 shows that\n     no configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines can exist.\n\nHence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid\nk\\text{ odd},\\;k\\le n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a smaller triangle\n\nAssume that for a given \\(n\\) we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\nChoose the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct and non\u2011sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); therefore\nall such points are already covered.  \nThe points that remain uncovered are those with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  Writing\n\\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and keeping the same \\(b\\), the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\na'+b\\le k+1,\\qquad a',b\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered set is exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a',b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhich is the same configuration as \\(S_k\\).  Consequently the original\nproblem is equivalent to the following reduced problem.\n\n> **Reduced problem.**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge1\\) decide whether the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered\n> by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A bound for a sunny line\n\nLet a sunny line have direction vector \\((p,q)\\) with coprime integers\n\\(p,q\\) and \\((p,q)\\notin\\{(1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\}\\) (up to sign).  \nThus \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge2\\).\n\nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) the lattice points on the line are spaced\nby \\(|p|\\) in the \\(a\\)-direction and by \\(|q|\\) in the \\(b\\)-direction.\nConsequently the number of lattice points of \\(T_k\\) that lie on a sunny line\ndoes not exceed the number of integer steps that fit into the interval of\nlength \\(k+1\\) when each step has length at least \\(2\\) in one coordinate.\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most }\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\text{ points of }T_k.}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---------------------------------------------