whether the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered\n> by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A bound for a sunny line\n\nLet a sunny line have direction vector \\((p,q)\\) with coprime integers\n\\(p,q\\) and \\((p,q)\\notin\\{(1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\}\\) (up to sign).  \nThus \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge2\\).\n\nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) the lattice points on the line are spaced\nby \\(|p|\\) in the \\(a\\)-direction and by \\(|q|\\) in the \\(b\\)-direction.\nConsequently the number of lattice points of \\(T_k\\) that lie on a sunny line\ndoes not exceed the number of integer steps that fit into the interval of\nlength \\(k+1\\) when each step has length at least \\(2\\) in one coordinate.\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most }\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\text{ points of }T_k.}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Even \\(k>0\\) are impossible\n\nLet \\(k=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  \nFrom (7) each sunny line covers at most \\(\\lceil\\frac{2m+1}{2}\\rceil=m\\) points.\nWith only \\(k\\) lines we can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot m = 2m\\cdot m = \\frac{k^{2}}2\n\\]\n\npoints.  However  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 = \\frac{2m(2m+1)}2 = m(2m+1) > m\\cdot 2m =\\frac{k^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the points of \\(T_k\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines when \\(k\\) is\neven and positive.  Consequently **no configuration with an even positive\nnumber of sunny lines exists**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge0\\)).  \nConsider the following \\(k\\) lines, all of slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y = x - i \\qquad &&(i=0,1,\\dots ,m),\\\\[2pt]\nM_j &: y = x + j \\qquad &&(j=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (8) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) and set \\(c=a-b\\).  \nFrom \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) we obtain \\(-m\\le c\\le m\\) (inequality\n(3) above).  \n\n*If \\(c\\ge0\\)*, then \\(c\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) and the point satisfies\n\\(b = a-c\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_c\\) of (8).  \n\n*If \\(c<0\\)*, write \\(c=-j\\) with \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\); then\n\\(b = a + j\\), so the point lies on the line \\(M_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines listed in (8),\nand the \\(k\\) lines cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Completion of the original problem\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  All are non‑sunny and\n  together they cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-k\\) (non‑sunny) to cover the\n  part of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \n  Translate the construction of Section 4 by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); i.e. replace\n  each line \\(y=x\\pm t\\) in (8) by the line \\(y = x\\pm t + (n-k)\\).  \n  These \\(k\\) translated lines are still sunny, distinct, and together they\n  cover the remaining points, which form the set \\(T_k\\).  Hence we obtain a\n  configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n* **\\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).**  \n  By the counting argument of Section 3 such a configuration cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\text{ is odd with }1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThese values are both necessary (even \\(k>0\\) are impossible) and sufficient\n(constructions are given above).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
