(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n       \\[\n       \\boxed{\\,a+2b = c+(n-k)\\qquad (c=3,4,\\dots ,k+2)\\,}.\n       \\]\n\n       Each of them is distinct, sunny, and together they cover precisely the points of the original set that were not covered by the vertical lines.  \n\n   Hence we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n4. **Extremal cases.**  \n   *   \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   *   \\(k=n\\): the construction above uses no vertical lines and the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(a+2b = 3,4,\\dots ,n+2\\).  \n\n   Thus every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is realizable.\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A reduction\n\nFix a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\).  \nPlace the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(V_a\\); therefore the points that are **not** covered by the family \\((1)\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nafter the translation \\(a\\mapsto a-(n-k)\\).  \nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following:\n\n> **Reduced problem.**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) find \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of the set \\(T_k\\) defined in \\((2)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with  \n\n\\[\n3\\le c\\le k+2,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ndefine  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\; a+2b=c .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Slope.*  Equation \\((4)\\) can be written \\(b=-\\tfrac12 a+\\tfrac{c}{2}\\); hence the slope is \\(-\\tfrac12\\).  \nSince \\(-\\tfrac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), every line \\(L_c\\) is sunny.\n\n*Distinctness.*  The constant term \\(c\\) is different for different values in \\((3)\\); consequently the lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=3}^{k+2}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_k\\).*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\),\n\n\\[\na+2b\\ge1+2\\cdot1=3 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\na+2b\\le (k+1-b)+2b = k+1+b\\le k+2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a+2b\\) is an integer belonging to the interval \\([3,k+2]\\), i.e. to the set \\(\\{3,4,\\dots ,k+2\\}\\).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies exactly one equation of the form \\((4)\\); that is, \\((a,b)\\) lies on a unique line \\(L_c\\) with \\(c\\) given by \\(c=a+2b\\).\n\nHence the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=3}^{k+2}\\) cover **all** points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lifting the construction to the original coordinates\n\nRecall that the points of \\(T_k\\) correspond to the points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) via the translation  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)\\in T_k\\;\\longleftrightarrow\\;(a+(n-k),\\,b)\\in S_n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nReplacing \\(a\\) by \\(a-(n-k)\\) in \\((4)\\) yields the lines in the original plane:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,a+2b = c+(n-k)\\qquad (c=3,4,\\dots ,k+2)\\,}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach line in \\((8)\\) is sunny (same slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)), the \\(k\\) lines are distinct, and by the argument of Section\u202f2 they cover precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) that are not already covered by the vertical lines \\((1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Verification of the whole configuration\n\nThe complete 