istinct lines that satisfies the two conditions of the problem and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   * \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable (steps\u202f3).  \n   * Every \\(k\\ge 3\\) is realizable by the construction of step\u202f4 together with the vertical lines of step\u202f1.  \n   * \\(k=2\\) is impossible (step\u202f2).  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to a triangular set  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nPlace the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\) lies on one of the lines (1).  \nConsequently the points that are **not** covered by the family (1) are exactly\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nafter the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a+(n-k),b)\\).  \nThus the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n> **Reduced problem.**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) find \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of the set \\(T_k\\) defined in (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nWhen \\(k=2\\),\n\n\\[\nT_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line through two of these points has slope  \n\n* \\(0\\) for \\((1,1)-(1,2)\\) (vertical),  \n* \\(\\infty\\) for \\((1,1)-(2,1)\\) (horizontal), or  \n* \\(-1\\) for \\((1,2)-(2,1)\\).\n\nAll three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line; therefore two sunny lines cannot cover the three points.  \nHence **no configuration exists for \\(k=2\\)**, for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n  All are non\u2011sunny and they cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only uncovered point of \\(S_n\\) is \\((n,1)\\).  \n  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  y=\\tfrac12\\,(x-n)+1\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  passes through \\((n,1)\\) and has slope \\(\\tfrac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny.  \n  Thus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Construction for every \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\ge3\\).  \nDefine the following \\(k\\) lines in the \\((a,b)\\)-plane.\n\n*For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k-2\\):*  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; a-b=i-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese are lines of slope \\(1\\) (sunny) and are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Two additional lines:*  \n\n\\[\nL_{k-1}:\\; a+(k-1)b = k+1 ,\\qquad\nL_{k}   :\\; (k-1)a + b = k+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes of \\(L_{k-1}\\) and \\(L_{k}\\) are \\(-\\dfrac1{k-1}\\) and \\(-\\,(k-1)\\) respectively; both are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so the two lines are sunny and distinct from the lines in (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.1  Verification that the \\(k\\) lines cover \\(T_k\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 \\(a\\ge b\\).*  \nThen \\(d:=a-b\\) is a non\u2011negative integer.  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le k+1\\),\n\n\\[\nd=a-b\\le a+b-2\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(d=k-1\\) then \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\) and \\(a=k\\); such a point satisfies \\(a+(k-1)b=k+1\\) and therefore lies on \\(L_{k-1}\\).  \nIf \\(d\\le k-2\\) then \\(d\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-2\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{d+1}\\) in (4).  \nHence every point with \\(a\\ge b\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\).\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 \\(a<b\\).*  \nWrite the defining inequality as \\(a\\le k+1-b\\) and multiply by \\(k-1\\):\n\n\\[\n(k-1)a\\le (k-1)(k+1-b)=k^2+k-(k-1)b .\n\\]\n\nAdding \\(b\\) to both sides gives  \n\n\\[\n(k-1)a+b\\le k+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause the left\u2011hand side is an integer, (6) implies either  \n\n\\[\n(k-1)a+b = k+1 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad (k-1)a+b\\le k .\n\\]\n\nIf equality holds, the point lies on \\(L_{k}\\) (the second line of (5)).  \nOtherwise \\((k-1)a+b\\le k\\).  Adding the inequality \\(a+(k-1)b\\ge 2\\) (which follows from \\(a,b\\ge1\\)) to the previous inequality yields  \n\n\\[\na+(k-1)b \\ge (k+1)-\\bigl[(k-1)a+b-(a+(k-1)b)\\bigr] \\ge k+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+(k-1)b\\) cannot exceed \\(k+1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>k+1\\)), we must have  \n\n\\[\na+(k-1)b = k+1 ,\n\\]\n\nso the point lies on \\(L_{k-1}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the \\(k\\) lines listed in (4)\u2013(5).  The lines are distinct and sunny, completing the covering of \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Lifting to the original set \\(S_n\\)\n\nRecall the vertical lines (1).  \nApply the translation  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)\\longmapsto (a+(n-k),\\,b)\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nto each of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \nThe resulting \\(k\\) lines are still sunny (translation does not change slope) and together with the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1) give a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n* covers every point of \\(S_n\\) (the points covered by (1) plus, via (7), the points of \\(T_k\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Final classification of admissible \\(k\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\) \u2013 realised by the \