ne through \\((1,1),(1,2)\\)),  \n* \\(\\infty\\) (vertical line through \\((1,1),(2,1)\\)),  \n* \\(-1\\) (line through \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)).\n\nAll three slopes are prohibited for a sunny line.  \nHence two sunny lines cannot cover the three points of \\(T_2\\).  \nTherefore **no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists** for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \nEach of them is non‑sunny, and together they contain every point of \\(S_n\\).  \nThus a configuration with **\\(k=0\\)** is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\) are covered.  \nThe only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\ny=\\tfrac12\\,(x-n)+1\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(\\tfrac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny and passes through \\((n,1)\\).  \nConsequently a configuration with **exactly one sunny line** exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Remarks on the case \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nFor \\(k=3\\) an explicit covering with three sunny lines can be exhibited, e.g.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: a-b=0,\\\\\nL_2 &: a+2b=5,\\\\\nL_3 &: 2a+b=5,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwhich together contain all points of \\(T_3\\).  \nHowever, extending this pattern to arbitrary \\(k\\ge4\\) proved non‑trivial.  \nAttempts to use the family of lines  \n\n\\[\na-b=i\\;(0\\le i\\le k-2),\\qquad\na+(k-1)b=k+1,\\qquad\n(k-1)a+b=k+1\n\\]\n\nfailed because the point \\((k,1)\\in T_k\\) is not covered when \\(k\\ge4\\).  \nOther natural families (e.g. lines of the form \\(a+ib=k+1\\) or \\((k-1)a+b=k+1\\)) also leave uncovered points.  \n\nAt present I have not succeeded in producing a construction that works for **every** \\(k\\ge3\\), nor have I been able to prove that such a construction is impossible.  \nConsequently the status of the values \\(k\\ge3\\) remains unresolved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Partial classification\n\nCombining the results of Sections 2–4 we obtain the following rigorous partial answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For any }n\\ge3,\\;k=0\\text{ and }k=1\\text{ are attainable, while }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\nThe existence of configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for the remaining integers \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) is not settled by the present argument.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
