            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to a triangular lattice\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nPlace the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\) lies on one of the lines (1).  \nConsequently the points that are still uncovered are exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nobtained from the original set by translating the first coordinate by \\(-(n-k)\\).  \nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following.\n\n> **Reduced problem.**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) find \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of the set \\(T_k\\) defined in (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. A counting lemma for sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{A sunny line contains at most }\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points of }T_k .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a sunny line in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  \nIntersect it with the triangle \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).  \nThe intersection is a line segment whose projection on the \\(a\\)-axis has length at most \\(\\frac{k+1}{|m+1|}\\) (the two boundary lines of the triangle have slopes \\(-1\\) and \\(0\\)).  \nIf the reduced slope of the line is \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(q>0\\), then successive lattice points on the line are spaced by \\((q,p)\\); therefore at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) of them can lie inside the triangle.  \nSince \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) for every sunny slope (the only slopes with \\(|p+q|=1\\) are \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)), we obtain the bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Even values of \\(k\\) are impossible\n\nAssume that \\(k\\) is even, write \\(k=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  \nThe set \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=\\frac{2m(2m+1)}2=m(2m+1)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line can cover at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor=m\\) points, hence \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot m =2m\\cdot m =2m^{2}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nBecause \\(2m^{2}<m(2m+1)\\) for every \\(m\\ge1\\), the inequality (4) is strictly smaller than (3).  \nTherefore for every even \\(k\\ge2\\) a covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).  \nConsider the family of lines\n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x-j\\qquad\\text{for }j=-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(1\\); consequently they are sunny.  \nThere are exactly \\(2m+1=k\\) distinct lines in the family.\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n-m\\le a-b\\le m .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\na-b\\ge a-(2m+2-a)=2a-(2m+2)\\ge -2m,\n\\]\nand similarly \\(a-b\\le 2m\\); the parity of \\(a+b\\) forces the tighter bound (6).  \nEquation (6) tells us that the integer difference \\(d:=a-b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{-m,\\dots ,m\\}\\).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(y=x-d\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_d\\) from (5).  \n\nThus **every** point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_{-m},L_{-m+1},\\dots ,L_{m}\\).  \nAll these lines are sunny and distinct, and their number is exactly \\(k\\). ∎\n\nFor the smallest odd case \\(k=1\\) the construction yields the single line \\(y=x\\); for \\(k=3\\) we obtain the three lines \\(y=x-1,\\;y=x,\\;y=x+1\\), which is precisely the explicit covering exhibited in the original attempt.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Completion of the original problem\n\nReturn to the original integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n* If \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are non‑sunny and they cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(k\\) is odd and \\(1\\le k\\le n\\), proceed as follows:  \n  – Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny).  \n  – Translate the set \\(T_k\\) back by adding \\(n-k\\) to the first coordinate; the covering supplied by Section 4 (the lines \\(y=x-j\\) with \\(-\\frac{k-1}{2}\\le j\\le\\frac{k-1}{2}\\)) becomes a set of \\(k\\) sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nAll lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Final answer\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nAll even values \\(k