the slopes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), which are forbidden),\n   we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n                =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\qquad\\blacksquare\n   \\]\n\n3. **Consequences of Lemma 1.**  \n\n   The set \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  If we have \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points\n   they can cover is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Even \\(k\\ge2\\).*  \n   Write \\(k=2m\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  Then (5) equals \\(2m\\cdot m=2m^{2}\\),\n   whereas (4) equals \\(m(2m+1)>2m^{2}\\).  Hence for every even\n   \\(k\\ge2\\) the reduced problem is impossible.\n\n   *Odd \\(k\\).*  \n   Write \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  Then (5) equals\n   \\((2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\), i.e. the bound of Lemma 1 is *tight*.\n   Consequently, to obtain a covering we must use lines that each\n   contain exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) points\n   of \\(T_k\\).\n\n4. **Construction for every odd \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge0\\)).  For each integer\n   \\(j\\in\\{-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\; y=-2x+(2m+2+j).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties of the lines.*\n\n   *   The slope is \\(-2\\); therefore each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n   *   The intercepts \\(2m+2+j\\) are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n   *   For a fixed \\(j\\) the integer points of \\(L_j\\) are\n       \\[\n       (x,\\, -2x+2m+2+j),\\qquad x=1,2,\\dots ,m+1 .\n       \\tag{7}\n       \\]\n       Indeed, the condition \\(y>0\\) forces \\(x\\le m+1\\), and the\n       condition \\(x+y\\le 2m+2\\) forces \\(x\\ge1\\).  Consequently each\n       line contains exactly \\(m+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n       points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Coverage proof.*  \n   Take any point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and\n   \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\).  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   j:=a+b-2a = b- a .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) and \\(a\\ge1\\), we have\n   \\(-m\\le j\\le m\\); hence the integer \\(j\\) belongs to the index set\n   of the lines (6).  Substituting \\(j=b-a\\) into (6) gives\n\n   \\[\n   b = -2a + (2m+2 + (b-a))\\;,\n   \\]\n\n   which is exactly the defining equation of the line \\(L_{\\,b-a}\\).\n   Therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{\\,b-a}\\).  Since every point of\n   \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_j\\), the family\n   \\(\\{L_j\\mid -m\\le j\\le m\\}\\) covers \\(T_k\\).\n\n   The family consists of \\(2m+1=k\\) distinct sunny lines, each of\n   which contains the maximal possible number of points, so the covering\n   is complete.\n\n5. **Putting the reduction back.**  \n\n   For a given \\(n\\ge3\\) and a non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\):\n\n   * If \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) vertical lines\n     \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none of them is sunny.\n   * If \\(k\\) is odd, we use the construction of step 4 for the set\n     \\(T_k\\) and add the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).\n     The vertical lines are non‑sunny, the \\(k\\) lines from step 4 are\n     sunny, and together they cover every point \\((a,b)\\) with\n     \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   By Lemma 1 even values \\(k\\ge2\\) are impossible, and the\n   constructions above show that every admissible value indeed occurs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a triangular lattice\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nPlace the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\) lies\non one of the lines (1).  The points that remain uncovered are precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nobtained from \\(S_n\\) by deleting the first \\(n-k\\) columns.\nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to the following.\n\n> **Reduced problem.**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) find \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose\n> union contains every point of the set \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A counting lemma for sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\quad\\text{for every sunny line }L .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a sunny line as  \n\n\\[\nax+by=c,\\qquad a,b,c\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ \\gcd(a,b)=1,\n\\]\n\nwith the three forbidden possibilities excluded:\n\\(b\\neq0\\) (otherwise the line is horizontal), \\(a\\neq0\\) (otherwise\nvertical), and \\(a+b\\neq0\\) (otherwise the slope is \\(-1\\)).\n\nAll integer points on the line are\n\n\\[\n(x_0,y_0)+t\\,(b,-a),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is any particular lattice point on the line.\nFor a point of the form (3) we have\n\n\\[\nx+y=(x_0+y_0)+t\\,(b-a).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\neq0\\), we have \\(b-a\\neq0\\).  Moreover,\n\\(|b-a|\\ge2\\); the only integer solutions of \\(|b-a|=1\\) are\n\\((a,b)=(1,0)\\) or \\((0,1)\\) (horizontal) and \\((a,b)=(1,-2)\\) or\n\\((-2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)), but in each case \\(a+b= \\pm1\\) which would\nforce the line to be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence \\(|b-a|\\ge2\\).\n\nAll points of \\(T_k\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\).  From (4) the\npossible values of the sum \\(x+y\\) on the line form an arithmetic\nprogression with step \\(|b-a|\\ge2\\); therefore the number of admissible\nterms is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{|b-a|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Consequences of Lemma 1\n\nThe set \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIf we have \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points they can cover\nis at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Even \\(k\\ge2\\).*  \nWrite \\(k=2m\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  Then (6) equals \\(2m\\cdot m=2m^{2}\\), whereas\n(5) equals \\(m(2m+1)>2m^{2}\\).  Hence for every even \\(k\\ge2\\) the\nreduced problem is impossible.\n\n*Odd \\(k\\).*  \nWrite \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  Then (6) equals\n\\((2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\); the bound of Lemma 1 is tight.\nConsequently a covering is possible **iff** we can exhibit \\(k\\) sunny\nlines each containing exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\)\npoints of \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for every odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).  For each integer  \n\n\\[\nj\\in\\{-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ndefine  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y=-2x+(2m+2+j).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Why the lines are sunny.*  \nAll have slope \\(-2\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore they are sunny.\n\n*Number of lattice points on a line.*  \nA point \\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(L_j\\) iff \\(y=-2x+2m+2+j\\).\nBecause \\(y>0\\) and \\(x+y\\le2m+2\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n1\\le x\\le m+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus each line \\(L_j\\) contains exactly the \\(m+1\\) points  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(x,\\,-2x+2m+2+j)\\mid x=1,2,\\dots ,m+1\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nall of which belong to \\(T_k\\).  Hence each line attains the bound of\nLemma 1.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_k\\).*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Set \\(j:=b-a\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le2m+2\\) and\n\\(a\\ge1\\), we have \\(-m\\le j\\le m\\); therefore \\(j\\) is one of the\nindices in (7).  Substituting \\(j=b-a\\) into (8) gives  \n\n\\[\nb=-2a+2m+2+(b-a),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equation of the line \\(L_{\\,b-a}\\).  Hence\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the lines\n\\(\\{L_j\\mid -m\\le j\\le m\\}\\).  The family consists of exactly\n\\(2m+1=k\\) distinct sunny lines, each containing \\(m+1\\) points of\n\\(T_k\\); consequently it is a required covering.  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be given.\n\n*If \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none is sunny and they\ncover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd and \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \nApply the construction of Section 4 to the set \\(T_k\\) (which is a\nright‑angled triangle of size \\(k\\)).  Add the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  The vertical lines are non‑sunny, the \\(k\\) lines\nfrom Section 4 are sunny, and together they cover every point of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge2\\).*  \nBy Lemma 1 and the counting argument of Section 3 such a covering is\nimpossible.\n\nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd},\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nAll these values are realizable, and no other non‑negative integer\n\\(k\\) satisfies the required conditions. ∎"
