ently a covering is possible **iff** we can exhibit \\(k\\) sunny\nlines each containing exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\)\npoints of \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for every odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).  For each integer  \n\n\\[\nj\\in\\{-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ndefine  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y=-2x+(2m+2+j).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Why the lines are sunny.*  \nAll have slope \\(-2\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore they are sunny.\n\n*Number of lattice points on a line.*  \nA point \\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(L_j\\) iff \\(y=-2x+2m+2+j\\).\nBecause \\(y>0\\) and \\(x+y\\le2m+2\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n1\\le x\\le m+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus each line \\(L_j\\) contains exactly the \\(m+1\\) points  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(x,\\,-2x+2m+2+j)\\mid x=1,2,\\dots ,m+1\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nall of which belong to \\(T_k\\).  Hence each line attains the bound of\nLemma 1.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_k\\).*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Set \\(j:=b-a\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le2m+2\\) and\n\\(a\\ge1\\), we have \\(-m\\le j\\le m\\); therefore \\(j\\) is one of the\nindices in (7).  Substituting \\(j=b-a\\) into (8) gives  \n\n\\[\nb=-2a+2m+2+(b-a),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equation of the line \\(L_{\\,b-a}\\).  Hence\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the lines\n\\(\\{L_j\\mid -m\\le j\\le m\\}\\).  The family consists of exactly\n\\(2m+1=k\\) distinct sunny lines, each containing \\(m+1\\) points of\n\\(T_k\\); consequently it is a required covering.  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be given.\n\n*If \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none is sunny and they\ncover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd and \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \nApply the construction of Section 4 to the set \\(T_k\\) (which is a\nright‑angled triangle of size \\(k\\)).  Add the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  The vertical lines are non‑sunny, the \\(k\\) lines\nfrom Section 4 are sunny, and together they cover every point of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge2\\).*  \nBy Lemma 1 and the counting argument of Section 3 such a covering is\nimpossible.\n\nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd},\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nAll these values are realizable, and no other non‑negative integer\n\\(k\\) satisfies the required conditions. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
