pes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), which are forbidden),\n   we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n                =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\qquad\\blacksquare\n   \\]\n\n3. **Consequences of Lemma 1.**  \n\n   The set \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  If we have \\(k\\) sunny lines, the total number of points\n   they can cover is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Even \\(k\\ge2\\).*  \n   Write \\(k=2m\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  Then (5) equals \\(2m\\cdot m=2m^{2}\\),\n   whereas (4) equals \\(m(2m+1)>2m^{2}\\).  Hence for every even\n   \\(k\\ge2\\) the reduced problem is impossible.\n\n   *Odd \\(k\\).*  \n   Write \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  Then (5) equals\n   \\((2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\), i.e. the bound of Lemma 1 is *tight*.\n   Consequently, to obtain a covering we must use lines that each\n   contain exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) points\n   of \\(T_k\\).\n\n4. **Construction for every odd \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge0\\)).  For each integer\n   \\(j\\in\\{-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\; y=-2x+(2m+2+j).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties of the lines.*\n\n   *   The slope is \\(-2\\); therefore each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n   *   The intercepts \\(2m+2+j\\) are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n   *   For a fixed \\(j\\) the integer points of \\(L_j\\) are\n       \\[\n       (x,\\, -2x+2m+2+j),\\qquad x=1,2,\\dots ,m+1 .\n       \\tag{7}\n       \\]\n       Indeed, the condition \\(y>0\\) forces \\(x\\le m+1\\), and the\n       condition \\(x+y\\le 2m+2\\) forces \\(x\\ge1\\).  Consequently each\n       line contains exactly \\(m+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n       points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Coverage proof.*  \n   Take any point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and