            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to a triangular lattice  \n\nFix a non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\).  \nConsider the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the lines \\(V_i\\).  \nThe points that are not covered by the family \\(\\{V_i\\}\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((a',b')=(a-(n-k),\\,b)\\).  \nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following reduced problem.\n\n> **Reduced problem.**  For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) find \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains every point of the set \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. A covering of the triangle by \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;y = x + j .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*The lines are sunny.*  Their slope is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n*The lines are distinct.*  Their intercepts are different.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\),\n\n\\[\n0\\le b-a\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b-a=j\\) for a unique integer \\(j\\) in the range \\([0,k-1]\\).  \nSubstituting \\(j=b-a\\) into (4) gives \\(b = a + (b-a)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_j\\).  \nTherefore \\((a,b)\\in L_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nT_k\\subseteq\\bigcup_{j=0}^{k-1} L_j .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) cover the whole triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Completion of the original configuration  \n\nTake the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \\(|\\mathcal F_k|=(n-k)+k=n\\).  \n*Sunny lines.*  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_j\\) are sunny.  \n*Coverage.*  A point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs to one of the two parts:\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\).  \n* If \\(a>n-k\\) write \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\;( \\ge1)\\).  \n  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\), i.e. \\((a',b)\\in T_k\\).  \n  By (5) the point \\((a',b)\\) lies on some \\(L_j\\); translating back, \\((a,b)\\) lies on the same line \\(L_j\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Exhaustion of the possible values of \\(k\\)\n\n*Existence.*  \nFor \\(k=0\\) the family \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) (all vertical) works.  \nFor each \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) constructed above works.  \nThus **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n*Non‑existence of other values.*  \nThe problem asks for non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of **exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines** exists.  \nSince we have only \\(n\\) lines, clearly \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\); also \\(k\\) cannot be negative.  \nTherefore no integer outside the interval \\([0,n]\\) can satisfy the conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll non‑negative integers not larger than \\(n\\) occur, and no other integer does.  \nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
