\), \\((k-1,1)\\), etc.) are **not** covered by any \\(L_j\\).  \n\n**Conclusion:** The argument that the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_0,\\dots,L_{k-1}\\) cover **all** of \\(T_k\\) is invalid. This is a **Critical Error** that breaks the existence proof for any \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\n### 5. Completion of the original configuration\n> \u201cTake the family  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal F_k=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}.\n> \\]  \n> \u2026  \n> Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- This conclusion directly depends on the claim that the \\(L_j\\) cover all points of \\(T_k\\).  \n- Since the covering claim is false, the statement that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers all of \\(S_n\\) is **unsubstantiated**.  \n- For concrete counterexample, let \\(n=4\\) and \\(k=2\\). Then \\(\\mathcal F_2\\) consists of vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) and sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=x+1\\). The point \\((4,1)\\) belongs to \\(S_4\\) (since \\(4+1=5\\le5\\)) but is not on any vertical line (its \\(x\\)-coordinate is 4) and not on either \\(y=x\\) or \\(y=x+1\\) (since \\(1\\neq4\\) and \\(1\\neq5\\)). Hence \\((4,1)\\) is uncovered.  \n\n**Conclusion:** The construction fails; the step is invalid due to the earlier critical error.\n\n### 6. Exhaustion of possible values of \\(k\\)\n> \u201cFor each \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) constructed above works.  \n> Thus **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  \n> \u2026  \n> Since we have only \\(n\\) lines, clearly \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\); also \\(k\\) cannot be negative.  \n> Therefore no integer outside the interval \\([0,n]\\) can satisfy the conditions.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The impossibility of \\(k>n\\) follows trivially from the fact that there are only \\(n\\) lines, so this part is correct.  \n- However, the existence claim for all \\(k\\) in \\([0,n]\\) rests on the flawed construction described in steps\u202f4\u20135. Since that construction does **not** actually cover all required points for any \\(k\\ge1\\), the existence claim is false.  \n\n**Conclusion:** The final answer is not justified; the solution\u2019s conclusion is invalid.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution\u2019s reduction to the sub\u2011problem is correct, but the subsequent construction of \\(k\\) sunny lines fails because it incorrectly assumes that every point of the triangle \\(T_k\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\ge0\\). This critical mistake invalidates the existence proof for all \\(k\\ge1\\), rendering the entire solution incorrect."
